00:05:43.780 --> 00:05:44.920
Let's talk about медведь.

00:05:45.340 --> 00:05:49.420
Did you know that the Russian word медведь и the English word honey

00:05:49.420 --> 00:05:54.420
are actually connected through a very old shared idea?

00:05:55.420 --> 00:05:58.580
They both come from ancient roots about sweetness.

00:05:59.020 --> 00:06:03.360
This word started long ago as something like millet for honey.

00:06:04.020 --> 00:06:08.780
It traveled through old languages and Russians created a polite nickname.

00:06:09.420 --> 00:06:13.700
They didn't want to say the scary animals real ancient name out loud.

00:06:14.420 --> 00:06:19.220
Instead they said медведь, the honey-nower or honey-eater.

00:06:19.520 --> 00:06:23.160
This is a classic nice way of saying something scary.

00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:26.800
English speakers sometimes say brewing instead of bear.

00:06:27.340 --> 00:06:28.620
The same idea.

00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:32.920
Next time you see the English word honey or taste mute,

00:06:33.620 --> 00:06:36.000
remember it is cousins with медведь.

00:06:36.200 --> 00:06:38.640
They both come from that ancient sweet root.

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:43.960
That connection is your secret shortcut to remembering the word forever.

00:06:45.580 --> 00:06:48.500
Russians have a very special relationship with bears.

00:06:48.500 --> 00:06:54.760
In Russian fairy tales and folklore, the bear is often called Михаил Патапыч,

00:06:54.940 --> 00:06:56.320
like a friendly uncle.

00:06:56.980 --> 00:07:01.180
He is seen as strong but a bit clumsy and lovable, not just scary.

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:05.980
You will find bear statues, toys and even Olympic mascots all over Russia.

00:07:06.620 --> 00:07:10.780
This warm storybook image shows how Russian culture turns powerful animals

00:07:10.780 --> 00:07:13.500
into family-like characters in bedtime stories.

